// SATB chorale: a four-part hymn texture on a grand staff, with TWO voices on
// EACH staff (soprano/alto on the treble, tenor/bass on the bass) via the
// voice { } { } span. Exercises polyphony on both staves of a grand staff at
// once, per-voice stem directions, and a cadential fermata. Authored in
// absolute octave (each voice sits in its own register) — which only works on
// a multi-part score once the file-level octave mode reaches every staff. The
// lyrics align to the soprano and sit under THAT staff, i.e. between the two —
// a lyric hangs off the staff it is attached to, inside a grand staff as much
// as anywhere else.
// ⚠️ THEY USED TO BE DRAWN BELOW THE WHOLE GRAND STAFF, and this comment used to
// say "Verified against LilyPond" about it. That claim had nothing behind it:
// `lysc ly` drops lyrics entirely, so this book's twin carries none and LilyPond
// was never asked. The rule that put them there was per-GROUP, and since a grand
// staff is one group it also collapsed an SATB score's four lines onto one
// baseline. See LyricStaffOrderTests.GrandStaffLyrics_SitUnderTheirOwnStaff.
octave absolute
title "Chorale"
composer "Lily#"

tempo 80
time 4/4
key g major

part upper { clef treble }
part lower { clef bass }

section Main {
  upper {
    voice { d'4 d' e' d' | b4 a  b2@fermata | }
    { b4  g  g  fis | g  fis g2 }
  }
  lower {
    voice { g,4  b,  c   a,  | d   d   d2 | }
    { g,,4 g,, c,  d,  | g,, d,  g,,2 }
  }
  lyrics words sings upper { Praise God from whom | all bless- ings | }
}

form main { Main }

score main "chorale" {
  grandStaff {
    staff upper with lyrics words
    staff lower
  }
}
